@end multitable

@item office_info
Describe a document without reading all of it: its format, what kind of document it is, its sheet or slide count, and its properties (title, author, dates). Cheap; prefer it before office_read when you only need to know what something is.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool office info}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{path} @tab string @tab yes @tab Absolute path to the document
@end multitable

@item office_outline
List a document's blocks with the anchors needed to edit them. Each line is INDEX, then the heading level or slide number, then the text. Pass an INDEX back to office_set_text to change that block -- this is how you locate what to edit.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool office outline}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{path} @tab string @tab yes @tab Absolute path to the document
@end multitable

@item office_read
Read the text of a Word document, spreadsheet or presentation -- .docx, .xlsx, .pptx, .odt, .ods or .odp. Returns the readable content: paragraphs for documents, tab-separated rows for spreadsheets, slide text for presentations. Use this instead of guessing at a file you cannot open.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool office read}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{path} @tab string @tab yes @tab Absolute path to the document
@end multitable

@item office_set_cell
Set one spreadsheet cell and save the file. REF is like "Sheet1!B7", or just "B7" for the first sheet. A VALUE beginning with `=' is stored as a formula. Everything else in the document is preserved byte-for-byte.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool office set-cell}
@item Surfaces: mcp, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@item Flags: destructive, confirm=ask
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{path} @tab string @tab yes @tab Absolute path to the spreadsheet
@item @code{ref} @tab string @tab yes @tab Cell reference, e.g. Sheet1!B7
@item @code{value} @tab string @tab yes @tab New value; a leading = makes it a formula
@end multitable

@item office_set_text
Replace the text of one block in a document, and save. INDEX comes from office_outline. For a presentation, pass the slide number as SLIDE too and INDEX is the shape's position on that slide. The block keeps its style; formatting that varied inside it is flattened. Nothing else in the document changes.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool office set-text}
@item Surfaces: mcp, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@item Flags: destructive, confirm=ask
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{path} @tab string @tab yes @tab Absolute path to the document
@item @code{index} @tab integer @tab yes @tab Block index from office_outline
@item @code{text} @tab string @tab yes @tab Replacement text
@item @code{slide} @tab integer @tab no @tab Slide number, for presentations only
@end multitable

@end table

@subheading schedule

@table @code
@item schedule_create
Create a scheduled task that runs a prompt on a cron schedule. Always call schedule_preview first and show the user what the expression means before creating it.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool schedule create}
@item Surfaces: mcp, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@item Flags: destructive, confirm=ask
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{title} @tab string @tab yes @tab Short description, used as the headline
@item @code{cron} @tab string @tab yes @tab Cron expression, e.g. '0 8 * * 1-5' or '@@daily'
@item @code{prompt} @tab string @tab yes @tab The prompt the agent runs each time it fires
@item @code{agent} @tab string @tab no @tab Agent definition to base it on
@item @code{model} @tab string @tab no @tab Provider/model, e.g. 'claude/claude-sonnet-4-6'
@item @code{tools} @tab string @tab no @tab Comma-separated tools the agent may use
@item @code{budget} @tab string @tab no @tab Spend ceiling per run, in dollars
@end multitable

@item schedule_delete
Delete a schedule permanently.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool schedule delete}
@item Surfaces: mcp, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@item Flags: destructive, confirm=ask
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{id} @tab string @tab yes @tab Schedule id, from schedule_list
@end multitable

@item schedule_list
List the user's scheduled agent tasks: what each one runs, on what cron expression, and when it next fires.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool schedule list}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@end itemize

Takes no arguments.

@item schedule_preview
Check a cron expression before using it: returns it in plain English plus the next few times it would fire. Use this to confirm an expression means what you intended.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool schedule preview}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{cron} @tab string @tab yes @tab Cron expression, e.g. '0 8 * * 1-5', '@@daily', '@@every 30m'
@item @code{count} @tab integer @tab no @tab How many upcoming times to show
@end multitable

@item schedule_set_enabled
Enable or disable an existing schedule without deleting it.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool schedule set-enabled}
@item Surfaces: mcp, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@item Flags: destructive, confirm=ask
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{id} @tab string @tab yes @tab Schedule id, from schedule_list
@item @code{enabled} @tab boolean @tab yes @tab Whether it should run
@end multitable

@end table

@subheading secondbrain

@table @code
@item secondbrain_find
Search the second brain for notes about a query: semantic when the memory index exists, text search otherwise. Returns paths, titles and snippets.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool secondbrain find}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{query} @tab string @tab yes @tab What to look for
@item @code{limit} @tab integer @tab no @tab Most results to return (default 10)
@end multitable

@item secondbrain_ingest
File a URL, a file (any format: PDF, Office, EPUB, mail, audio, video, YouTube, HTML, Markdown, data) or literal text into the user's second brain as an Org note: summarised, tagged, placed in the PARA tree, linked to related notes and registered in its index. Returns the job's final status as JSON, including note_file. Slow for media (transcription) and long documents (summary); expect tens of seconds.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool secondbrain ingest}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@item Flags: async (timeout 900s)
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{input} @tab string @tab yes @tab A URL, an absolute path, or empty when text is given
@item @code{text} @tab string @tab no @tab Literal text to ingest instead of a file or URL
@item @code{para} @tab string @tab no @tab inbox, projects, areas, resources or detect (default: configured placement)
@item @code{category} @tab string @tab no @tab Sub path under the PARA category, e.g. technical/linux
@item @code{tags} @tab string @tab no @tab Comma-separated tags to add
@item @code{summary_type} @tab string @tab no @tab Summary template: general, meeting, book, youtube, ... or auto
@item @code{title} @tab string @tab no @tab Title to use instead of the material's own
@item @code{no_summary} @tab boolean @tab no @tab Skip the AI summary
@end multitable

@item secondbrain_ingest_status
Status of an ingest job by id, as JSON.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool secondbrain ingest-status}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{id} @tab string @tab yes @tab Job id, e.g. sbi-3
@end multitable

@item secondbrain_tree
List the PARA notes tree as root-relative paths. Use this to see where a note could be filed before choosing a category.

@itemize @bullet
@item CLI: @command{emacsctl tool secondbrain tree}
@item Surfaces: mcp, dbus, cli, menu, agents
@item Returns: string
@end itemize

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .12 .1 .58
@headitem Argument @tab Type @tab Required @tab Description
@item @code{para} @tab string @tab no @tab Restrict to one category: inbox, projects, areas, resources, archives
@item @code{category} @tab string @tab no @tab Sub path under that category
@item @code{files} @tab boolean @tab no @tab Include .org files, not only directories
@end multitable

@end table

